JMM has really 5 sets of rules to control reordering:
<a> monitor enter/ volatile loads use acquire semantics

<b> monitor exit/volatile stores use release semantics

<c> dont reorder loads around causal stores (aka use common sense)

<d> There is a Total Lock Order

<e> There is a Total Volatile Store Order

<a> and <b> are easy to explain look at the following picture, the intention is to make sure
memops dont escape the box from inside.


 _______________________________________    ----------> Acquire Semantics  (Load)
|					|
|					|   Instr X
|					|
|_______________________________________|
                                            ----------> Release Semantics  (Store)

So X shouldnt be able to escape up so use Load[X] barrier and X shouldnt be able to escape down so 
use [X]Store barrier (replace X with Load or Store based on what memop it is)

----- Thats it !!  Meaning feel free to reorder loads / stores as much as you like ------------------------

============================================================================================
  Where do I place the membars? (Perspective 2)
============================================================================================
This is another perspective for understanding the JMM viz., to understand where the barriers
need to be placed.

There are really 4 entities:
-> Loads  (L)
-> Stores (S)
-> Monitor Enter / Volatile Loads  (N)
-> Monitor Exit  / Volatile Stores (X)

Barriers are required to be placed after N and X. When placing these barriers keep the following rule in mind
and you ll do fine.

L  L  S  N  N  then between N and N place the LoadLoad barrier.

============================================================================================
  PERSPECTIVE 3 --- Simple Rules  { Box / Total / Publication / Thread }
============================================================================================

<1> Dont escape the Box
    <a> and <b> explained above

<2> Total Lock / Total Volatile Store order
    <d> and <e> meaning if you see MeX followed by MeN use membar #StoreLoad

<3> Thread start has MeN and Thread join has MeX semantics

<4> Publication Safety:
    -> final stores not reordered with stores outside ctor function
    -> If ctor inlined stores on object reference not reordered with final field stores

    [Keep in mind non final fields are not guaranteed to be *not* reordered]
    [Also another reason why this should not escape until ctor completes else escaped
     ref may not see all final writes]

